  "meta": {
    "title": "Sipser’s M₁: w#w",
    "blurb": "Zig-zags across the # crossing off matching symbols: remember the first unmarked symbol in the state, find its partner on the other side, cross both off, come back. The machine from Sipser’s Example 3.9, state for state.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "#",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "ε#ε"
      },
      {
        "w": "0#0",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "101#101",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "0110#0110",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "01#10",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "1#11",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "10#1",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "0#0#0",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "sipser",
        "copy-language",
        "textbook",
        "not-context-free"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "chapter": "Sipser, Introduction to the Theory of Computation, Example 3.9",
      "readme": "Sipser's informal description of M₁ — \"zig-zag across the tape to corresponding positions on either side of the # symbol to check whether these positions contain the same symbol\" — and the formal state diagram behind it, rebuilt here with his numbering.\n\n- **q2** and **q3** carry the symbol just crossed off (0 or 1) to the right side.\n- **q4** and **q5** skip what has already been matched there.\n- **q6** and **q7** walk back to the leftmost unmatched symbol.\n- **q8** is the final check that nothing on the right is left over.\n\nThe run takes O(n²) steps, one round trip per symbol. **B = {w#w}** is not context-free, so no pushdown automaton could do this; a queue automaton can, and in linear time — compare **Perfect copy: w#w**."
    }
  }
}
